# 4. SCENE 04 — THE ORBITAL HUB

After the initial portal journey, the visitor emerges into the central universe of Forkframe Studio.

This is the main navigation environment and one of the most important pieces of the project.

It must feel expansive, carefully composed and spatially believable.

The camera exits the tunnel into a large architectural chamber or orbital environment.

A central structure anchors the scene.

Around it, several destinations occupy different positions in three-dimensional space.

The destinations should not look like conventional website cards floating in front of a background.

They must feel like physical or holographic structures existing within the environment.

Possible destinations include:

* A cinematic services structure.
* A portfolio gallery.
* A social-content creation studio.
* An interactive project calculator.
* A project enquiry and briefing environment.
* An insights and knowledge library.
* A studio information environment.
* A secure administration portal, available only through authenticated access.

Each destination should have its own recognisable silhouette, lighting treatment and spatial identity.

### Navigation objects

Use combinations of architectural gateways, geometric installations, suspended structures, rotating models, luminous rings and other appropriate 3D objects.

Do not use identical spheres for every destination.

The visitor should be able to recognise the general purpose of a destination before entering it.

Provide short, readable labels and concise descriptions using accessible interface elements associated with the relevant world-space objects.

On hover or keyboard focus:

* The object responds.
* Its lighting changes subtly.
* Its label becomes clearer.
* A short description appears.
* The visitor receives a clear indication that it is interactive.

On selection:

1. The object responds visually.
2. A selection sound plays if enabled.
3. The environment acknowledges the destination.
4. The camera turns toward the selected structure.
5. The structure activates.
6. The visitor enters the high-speed tunnel.
7. The camera exits into the chosen environment.

**The tunnel must connect the spaces. It must not be a disconnected animation that plays while the application abruptly swaps content behind it.**

Prepare destination assets before departure when practical, so the journey can finish without a loading pause.

For heavier destinations, use a deliberate preparation phase and show an elegant loading indicator if required.

Do not conceal a failed load behind an endless animation.

### Hub navigation requirements

Visitors must always be able to:

* Return to the hub.
* Navigate to another destination.
* Open a destination directly through its URL where appropriate.
* Use browser Back and Forward.
* Identify their current location.
* Escape a cinematic transition when safe.
* Reach important functionality without being trapped in the 3D environment.

The hub is a visual navigation layer, not the only way to access the website.

