# 6. SPATIAL DESIGN SYSTEM

Create a reusable design system for the entire universe.

## Environment composition

Use actual 3D geometry for major structures, gateways, portal rings, spatial frames and focal installations.

Use a mixture of modelled geometry, instanced objects, procedural elements and properly licensed assets.

Do not make every object procedurally complex if a simpler solution produces a better result.

Maintain a consistent sense of scale, horizon, camera perspective, lighting and spatial orientation.

## Materials

Develop a restrained, coherent material library:

* Deep matte surfaces.
* Brushed or polished metal.
* Dark architectural glass.
* Controlled emissive materials.
* Carefully tuned translucent surfaces.
* Premium neutral materials for physical objects.
* Limited accent colours to distinguish destinations.

Glass must not simply be a transparent object with a bright border.

Where glass is used, tune transparency, reflectivity, roughness, lighting and background contrast to create a convincing effect.

Use physically based materials when appropriate, but do not use expensive transmission and reflection effects indiscriminately.

## Lighting

Use lighting to establish depth and guide the visitor.

Implement carefully controlled:

* Key lighting.
* Rim lighting.
* Environment illumination.
* Emissive accents.
* Directional tunnel lighting.
* Destination-specific illumination.
* Contact shadows where useful.

Avoid dozens of unnecessary real-time lights.

Use baked lighting, environment maps, selective shadow rendering and other optimisation techniques where they improve quality without undermining the visual objective.

## Typography

Use a carefully selected typographic system.

Prioritise:

* Readability.
* Strong hierarchy.
* Controlled line lengths.
* Consistent spacing.
* Legible labels at different viewport sizes.
* Suitable contrast.
* A coherent relationship between the 3D environment and interface typography.

Load only necessary font weights and styles.

Ensure text remains crisp and readable on mobile devices.

## Spatial interface components

Create reusable components for:

* Destination labels.
* Object-selection indicators.
* World-space navigation prompts.
* Contextual information panels.
* Tooltips.
* Loading states.
* Progress indicators.
* Modal or focused interaction layers.
* Audio controls.
* Navigation history.
* Error and recovery states.

Use stable design tokens for colour, spacing, type, motion, materials and interaction states.

