# 7. THE REAL-TIME 3D ENGINE

Choose an appropriate real-time 3D stack after assessing the project requirements.

A suitable default for a modern React application is:

* Next.js with TypeScript.
* React Three Fiber.
* Three.js.
* Drei where useful.
* A suitable animation library such as GSAP or a well-designed native animation system.
* A dedicated state machine or transition controller for complex navigation.
* Web Audio API or an appropriate audio library.
* A suitable post-processing pipeline used selectively.

These are recommendations, not reasons to install unnecessary dependencies. Verify current compatibility and choose a maintainable implementation.

## Scene architecture

Separate the application into understandable systems:

* Scene manager.
* Camera controller.
* Navigation and transition controller.
* Asset manager.
* Lighting and environment system.
* Audio manager.
* Interaction and raycasting system.
* Quality and performance manager.
* Destination environment modules.
* Accessible DOM interface.
* Route and browser-history integration.

Avoid a single enormous component containing the entire universe.

Use clear TypeScript types for scene definitions, destination metadata, interaction states and transition configuration.

## World-space interaction

Implement reliable pointer interaction with real 3D objects.

Support:

* Hover.
* Selection.
* Focus indication.
* Click and tap.
* Raycasting.
* Interaction locking during transitions.
* Touch input.
* Keyboard-accessible alternatives.
* Clear hit areas.

Do not make users click tiny decorative meshes.

The visible object and its interactive area should align sensibly.

## Animation quality

Use a consistent motion system.

Animation should have a purpose and a clear beginning, middle and end.

Create shared motion settings for:

* Object activation.
* Hover response.
* Selection.
* Camera turns.
* Tunnel acceleration.
* Destination arrival.
* Environmental idle movement.
* Panel appearance.
* Error recovery.

Prevent conflicting animations from fighting over the same camera or object.

Cancel, complete or reconcile active animations when the user changes direction.

## Asset loading

Implement a robust asset pipeline.

Requirements:

* Load only the assets needed for the current experience.
* Preload likely destinations when resources allow.
* Lazy-load heavy environments.
* Reuse shared assets.
* Dispose of unused GPU resources when appropriate.
* Cache assets sensibly.
* Handle loading failures.
* Avoid blocking the entire interface because a decorative asset failed.
* Provide an understandable fallback when WebGL is unavailable.

The initial experience must not download the entire universe before showing anything useful.

## Browser lifecycle

Handle:

* Page visibility changes.
* Browser tab suspension.
* WebGL context loss and recovery where supported.
* Window resizing.
* Orientation changes.
* Reduced device capability.
* Asset-loading failures.
* Re-entry after navigation.
* Unmounting and disposal of scene resources.

Do not assume that a WebGL context will remain available indefinitely.

