# 9. ACCESSIBILITY WITHOUT DESTROYING THE VISION

An immersive website must remain usable by people who cannot or do not want to navigate through a 3D environment.

Implement an accessible semantic interface alongside the spatial experience.

Requirements:

* Keyboard navigation.
* Visible focus states.
* Semantic headings and landmarks.
* Accessible names for interactive destinations.
* Screen-reader-accessible destination descriptions.
* Accessible forms and validation.
* Sufficient text contrast.
* Appropriate focus management.
* Reduced-motion support.
* Sound controls.
* A way to bypass or shorten cinematic introductions.
* A conventional, accessible route to important content and forms.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

When reduced motion is enabled, replace fast tunnel travel with a shorter, calmer spatial transition or a direct destination change.

Do not force visitors through high-speed camera movement.

The 3D scene itself cannot serve as the only representation of the site's information. Provide accessible DOM-based alternatives for navigation and essential content.

Avoid scroll hijacking.

Do not prevent normal browser navigation or make it impossible to reach a destination without using a mouse.

