# 22. VISUAL ACCEPTANCE TESTS

Use these specific visual checkpoints throughout development.

## Checkpoint A — Initial arrival

Inspect the actual browser at desktop and mobile sizes.

Verify that the desk and laptop are properly composed, the environment has depth, the laptop is recognisable and the initial interaction is discoverable.

Reject the scene if the laptop is cropped incorrectly, looks like a flat image or appears too small to serve as the primary focus.

## Checkpoint B — Laptop activation

Verify the activation response, screen transformation and camera alignment.

Reject abrupt transitions that make it appear that an unrelated page has loaded.

## Checkpoint C — Tunnel departure

Verify that the camera actually enters the tunnel.

Inspect perspective, acceleration, object motion, lighting, directional streaks and sound timing.

Reject a tunnel that looks like a static image with moving particles layered over it.

## Checkpoint D — High-speed travel

Verify that the tunnel feels fast and three-dimensional.

Check that the vanishing point, motion layers, geometry and camera direction agree.

Reject motion that feels like a sideways slide, random zoom or flat transition.

## Checkpoint E — Arrival at the hub

Verify that the camera decelerates into the correct position.

The hub must be immediately understandable and visually composed.

Reject arrivals that end with the camera inside geometry, facing away from the environment or looking at an empty space.

## Checkpoint F — Destination travel

Select several different destinations.

Verify that each transition has a clear departure, meaningful travel and correct arrival.

Reject destinations that all look identical or open without a coherent spatial journey.

## Checkpoint G — Functional interaction

Test forms, calculators, saved projects and administration through the actual interface.

Reject visual controls that do not perform their advertised actions.

## Checkpoint H — Small screens

Test portrait mobile, landscape mobile and tablet layouts.

Reject scenes with inaccessible objects, unreadable labels, broken camera composition or unusable touch targets.

